Let f(x, y) = x² + 2y². 
Then (∂f)/(∂y) = 4y, both of which are comtinuous functions on all of R². 
Thus, Theorm 3.5 implies that f is differentiable everywhere. 
The surface z = x² + 2y² must therefore have a tangent plane at every point.
At  the point (2, -1), for example, this tangent plane is given by the equation

		z = 6 + 4(x - 2) - 4(y + 1)

(or, equivalently, by 4x - 4y - z = 6)

While we're on the subject of continuity and differentiablility, the next result is the multivariable analogue of a familiar theorem about function of one variable. 

